# Frontend and UX rules

## Plain language

Users are ordinary people, not lawyers. The interface must never assume legal vocabulary.

Write "Tell us what happened", not "Select the applicable jurisprudential cause of
action". Give every category a plain-language description. Avoid intimidating forms —
start with a few fields and request more only when needed.

The intake screen makes three things clear: users can describe the issue in their own
words, AI will help organise the request, and a legal professional remains responsible for
professional advice.

## Legal-safety boundary

AI-assisted results are presented as organisational help, never as legal advice, a
verdict, or a prediction. Keep the disclaimer clear but unobtrusive:

> AI-assisted results are provided to help organise your request and connect you with an
> appropriate legal professional. They are not a substitute for professional legal advice.

Show matching explanations to the user — recommendations are transparent, never "the best
lawyer for you".

## Screens

Only build screens a requirement or workflow calls for.

**Public** — landing, lawyer directory and profile, login/register
**Citizen** — dashboard, describe legal issue, triage result/confirm category, recommended
lawyers, consultation request (time slot, Google Calendar, Google Meet), my requests, account (name, phone, and password)
**Lawyer** — dashboard, profile, incoming requests, request detail with intake summary,
account
**Admin** — dashboard, users, lawyer profiles, subscription plans, legal categories, account

## Code

Keep business rules out of components — call the API and render. Frontend validation
mirrors backend rules for UX but is never the authority. Never call the AI provider
directly or hold a provider key in client code; all AI goes through the backend.

Handle loading, empty, and error states for every async view, especially the AI triage
step, which can fall back or take time. An AI fallback shows a controlled message, never a
raw error.
